rulings: ["[{'date': '2017-01-11', 'text': 'This card recieved errata for its flavor text on Gatherer: http://gatherer.wizards.com/Pages/Card/Details.aspx?multiverseid=73979'}]", "[{'date': '2017-01-11', 'text': 'This card recieved errata for its flavor text on Gatherer: http://gatherer.wizards.com/Pages/Card/Details.aspx?multiverseid=74275'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-15', 'text': 'The flavor text unscrambles to “Did you go through all the trouble to unscramble this?“'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'A permanent card that becomes the top card of your library enters the battlefield (unless it went from the battlefield to the top of your library), so any enters-the-battlefield abilities will trigger and “as [this creature] enters the battlefield” abilities will apply. Similarly, a permanent card that stops being the top card of your library leaves the battlefield (unless it went from the top of your library to the battlefield directly).'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If a creature you control that isn’t part of your library is put on top of your library, surprisingly little happens, at least at first. That creature doesn’t leave or enter the battlefield. It just stays on the battlefield. Of course, now that it’s also the top card of your library, you’ll probably draw it soon. This is also true if something puts the top card of your library directly onto the battlefield, without you having to play it.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card is your library is an Aura card, that Aura is on the battlefield not attached to anything. As such, it’s put into your graveyard. The new top card of your library is then revealed.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature card and it’s put into your graveyard, the creature did die. Note that it wasn’t destroyed or sacrificed or anything. It just died. <sad trombone noises>'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature card, it can attack. It does have haste, after all. It can also block.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature, planeswalker, land, enchantment, or artifact card, it’s also on the battlefield. Ta-da! This is, as they say, very weird.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a permanent card with an activated ability that requires it to be tapped, that ability can be activated. Land cards on top of your library can be tapped for mana, and so on.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are instructed to draw multiple cards, you draw them one at a time. Each card you draw after the first becomes the top card of your library briefly and is revealed. Each permanent card revealed this way enters and then leaves the battlefield, for any abilities that care about that.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'All nine rows count. You can fill rows horizontally, vertically, and diagonally. A full card with give B-I-N-G-O +72/+72.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Casting multiple spells with the same mana value has no additional benefit. Once a spot has a chip counter, it’s marked, and additional chip counters won’t do anything.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The counters are considered to be on the inherent tracker in B-I-N-G-O’s art, not on the creature itself. Those counters can’t be removed or moved to another permanent. Similarly, if you proliferate, you can’t add more chip counters to B-I-N-G-O.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Any even number of Topsy Turvys (Turvies?) will essentially cancel each other out and return the order of phases to their normal order.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Steps within each phase play out as normal. For example, although the beginning phase happens last in a turn, the untap, upkeep, and draw steps happen in their traditional order. (If it were the other way, combat would require psychics to get through.)'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Avatar of Me is only the color of your eyes, not the color of your eyes and blue. That is, the color it gets from its last ability overrides the blue it gets from its mana cost.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If each of your eyes is a different color (naturally or via a single contact lens), Avatar of Me is both colors.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re wearing colored contact lenses, Avatar of Me is whatever color your eyes appear to be. You can add or remove contact lenses to change your eye color in response to spells and abilities.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Silver-bordered rules include any conceivable eye color, not just the five traditional Magic colors. If your eye are hazel, Avatar of Me is hazel.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Thanks to Avatar of Me, if you’re asked to choose a color in a silver-bordered game, you can name any color. “That dreamy shade of blue found only in the sky on a perfect afternoon… and in your eyes” is acceptable, but a little weird.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'By default, you are limited to cards legal in the format you’re playing. If you’re playing Draft or Sealed Deck, you can name any card available in the format you’re playing. If you’re playing with Unsanctioned decks, anything goes. Discuss with your playgroup if you’d prefer to modify or eliminate these limitations.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Each Richard tracks its own list of played cards. If one leaves the battlefield and another enters, cards previously played under the first Richard are available once again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If more than one player controls a Richard Garfield, Ph.D., the cards one player plays has no effect on the other players. For example, each player could play Savannah Lions as a Swords to Plowshares this way.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Playing cards as though they were other cards is optional.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Richard Garfield, Ph.D. essentially makes you play a variant of the Magic game called “Mental Magic.” Here’s how the game works. Whenever you cast a spell, you reveal the card to all players and choose any other card (see below for the cards that you can use) that has that card’s mana cost. (Note that color very much does matter.) That spell can then be cast as though it was the chosen card. You aren’t allowed to choose the same card twice.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The same is true if Richard Garfield, Ph.D. changes controllers. If you play a Savannah Lions as Swords to Plowshares, then an opponent gains control of Richard Garfield, Ph.D., that player can then do the same.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Cheatyface’s mana cost is {U}{U}{U}.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must legally get Cheatyface into your hand before you can attempt to sneak it onto the battlefield. For example, you can’t sneak it in from your sideboard or collection during a game.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Due to changes in how echo and phasing work since Old Fogey was originally printed, echo mostly does nothing in this case. This is, as they say, a bit weird. Old Fogey is phased out when its echo ability would trigger, so that ability doesn’t trigger at the beginning of the upkeep after Old Fogey enters the battlefield. Phasing in doesn’t cause Old Fogey to enter the battlefield, so echo ends up not really doing much at all.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Duh can target a creature token only if the object you’re using to represent the token has reminder text, even if it’s a copy of a creature with reminder text.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Look at the actual creature you’re hoping to destroy to see if it has reminder text. Its Oracle text doesn’t matter for this purpose. It also doesn’t matter if another printing of the creature has (or doesn’t have) reminder text.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Emcee’s ability will trigger whenever a creature enters the battlefield under an opponent’s control. You choose whether you announce its arrival or not.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are unable (not just unwilling) to use a deep, booming voice, you are free to make accommodations in the spirit of Emcee. Sign overly dramatically, for example.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you control more than one Emcee, each ability triggers separately. You have to announce the creature’s entrance each time. Okay, you can just stay standing if you want.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You may add in nicknames for the creatures you announce, but you must also get the creature’s actual name in there to count. For example, “Gary, the Gray Merchant of Asphodel” is fine, but simply “Gary” wouldn’t work.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Flashback and buyback don’t mix. If you cast Blast from the Past using flashback, it will be exiled rather than go back to your hand.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Flashback and kicker do mix. You can pay the kicker cost in addition to the flashback cost to get the extra Goblin token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you cycle Blast from the Past, you can then use madness to cast it. If you cast Blast from the Past using madness, you may also pay for kicker or buyback.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Frankie Peanuts hates snitches, but he detests unsporting conduct. Keep your questions appropriate for your game and its surroundings.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re the player answering the yes-or-no question, you must abide by your answer if able. For example, if you are asked “Is the Slaying Mantis you control going to attack on your next turn?” and you say yes, then it must attack if able. However, if it’s tapped as you declare attackers that turn, then you aren’t able to attack with it, and you don’t have to abide by your answer.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Enter the Dungeon is cast during a subgame, a sub-subgame begins, preferably under a new, smaller table, but do what you can. The winner of the sub-sub game would search their subgame library for two cards.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you choose 0 for the first number, you are allowed to choose -1 for the second. The same is true for ½ and -½.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Look at Me, I’m R&D applies to all numbers that appear on the card, including numbers in mana symbols, rules text, power, toughness, collector number, and art.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Note that you are changing numbers, not numerals within a number. Choosing 2 and 3 won’t change 20 to 30 or ½ to ⅓.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can choose a number with a fraction.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you create a token that’s a copy of Pointy Finger of Doom, spin whatever object you’re using to represent the token. Do not use living beings to represent the token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Once the Finger starts spinning, no permanents can be moved until after the ability completely resolves.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pointy Finger of Doom must rotate at least 360 degrees to count. There is no penalty for failing to spin it properly other than you must try again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pointy Finger of Doom won’t destroy itself, even though it is very close to itself.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you have multiple copies in your library while searching it, you may reveal all of them, provided you can pay the {1}{B} for each of them.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you search your library multiple times in the same turn, each Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil in that library can be revealed once that turn. You may reveal multiples to prove you have multiples.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil doesn’t itself allow you to search your library. You must have another spell or ability that instructs you to search it.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can reveal Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil any time while searching your library before you shuffle it.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'In a game with more than two players, players can use paper and pencil to track where their pop! counter is. That is, the tracker located in Water Gun Balloon Game’s art is fun, but not required to work.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pink joins gold and every eye color imaginable in the set of colors in silver-bordered Magic games.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The pop! counters are considered to be on the inherent tracker in Water Gun Balloon Game’s art, not on the permanent itself. Those counters can’t be removed or moved to another permanent. Similarly, if you proliferate, you can’t add more pop! counters to Water Gun Balloon Game.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can open booster packs that contain cards not legal in the format you’re playing.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use any sealed Magic booster pack, including promo packs. As long as it hasn’t previously been opened and you don’t know the contents beforehand, you’re good.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must say the flavor text out loud or use a comparable method of communicating. For example, sign language is allowed, but if you sign the flavor text, you are considered to have spoken. Goblin Mime will notice.'}]"]
